<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
Let's break down the comment and translate it into an assert statement.

The comment states:
"If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then for every valid index from this index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null and the old element at this index is null, or the element at the next position equals the old element at this index."

This can be translated into the following logic:
- We need to check for every valid index `i` starting from `index` to `\old(this.size()) - 1` (inclusive).
- For each such index `i`, we need to check that either:
  - The element at position `i+1` is `null` and the element at position `i` was `null` before the insertion, or
  - The element at position `i+1` equals the element at position `i` before the insertion.

In JML (Java Modeling Language) style, this can be expressed using a universal quantifier (`\forall`) and logical conditions.

Here is the assert statement:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>

However, since `get(i+1)` might throw an `IndexOutOfBoundsException` when `i` is at the last index, we need to adjust the condition to avoid this. We can do this by changing the upper bound of the loop to `\old(this.size()) - 1`.

So, the corrected assert statement is:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()) - 1; (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>

But, to cover the edge case when `index` is equal to `\old(this.size())`, we need to add an additional condition to handle the last element separately:

<code>assert (\forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()) - 1; (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) && (index == \old(this.size()) || (this.get(index) == null && \old(this.get(index)) == null));</code>

However, the simpler and more direct way to express this is:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (i == \old(this.size()) - 1 || (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>

This ensures that we correctly handle the edge case when `i` is the last index.

Thus, the final assert statement is:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (i == \old(this.size()) - 1 || (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
